\section{Shock-Layer Chemistry Appendix}
\label{appendix:shock-layer-chemistry}

\subsection{Overview}
During hypersonic entry at $v_{\text{entry}} \approx 22.4~\mathrm{km/s}$,
the UAP-01 atmospheric probe encounters extreme shock-layer conditions
characterized by high-temperature dissociation, ion formation,
nonequilibrium radiation, and rapid chemical energy exchange. This
appendix derives the governing chemical kinetics, reaction pathways, and
energy transfer mechanisms relevant to Uranus’s hydrogen–helium–methane
atmosphere.

\subsection{Shock-Layer Thermochemical State}
Post-shock temperature:
\[
T_2 \sim 10{,}000~\mathrm{K},
\]
drives extensive dissociation of major species:
\[
\text{H}_2,\; \text{He},\; \text{CH}_4,\; \text{NH}_3,\; \text{C}_x\text{H}_y.
\]

The shock-layer mixture is modeled using a multi-species,
nonequilibrium chemical system:
\[
\frac{dY_i}{dt} = \dot{\omega}_i,
\]
where $Y_i$ is species mass fraction and $\dot{\omega}_i$ is net
production rate.

\subsection{Primary Dissociation Reactions}
Major dissociation pathways include:
\[
\text{H}_2 + M \rightarrow 2\text{H} + M,
\]
\[
\text{CH}_4 + M \rightarrow \text{CH}_3 + \text{H} + M,
\]
\[
\text{CH}_4 + M \rightarrow \text{CH}_2 + 2\text{H} + M,
\]
\[
\text{NH}_3 + M \rightarrow \text{NH}_2 + \text{H} + M,
\]
where $M$ is a collision partner.

Rate coefficients follow Arrhenius form:
\[
k(T) = A T^n \exp\left(-\frac{E_a}{RT}\right),
\]
with activation energies $E_a$ derived from UUAS THERMO-Q2.

\subsection{Ion Formation and Plasma Chemistry}
High shock temperatures generate significant ionization:
\[
\text{H} + \text{H}_2^+ \rightarrow \text{H}_3^+,
\]
\[
\text{H}_3^+ + e^- \rightarrow \text{H}_2 + \text{H},
\]
\[
\text{C}_x\text{H}_y + e^- \rightarrow \text{C}_x\text{H}_y^+ + 2e^-.
\]

Electron temperature $T_e$ evolves according to:
\[
\frac{dT_e}{dt}
= \frac{Q_{\text{Joule}} - Q_{\text{rad}} - Q_{\text{coll}}}{n_e k_B},
\]
where:
\begin{itemize}
    \item $Q_{\text{Joule}}$ is electrical heating,
    \item $Q_{\text{rad}}$ is radiative cooling,
    \item $Q_{\text{coll}}$ is collisional energy exchange.
\end{itemize}

UUAS ION-Q4 provides $Q_{\text{rad}}$ and $Q_{\text{coll}}$ profiles.

\subsection{Carbon-Bearing Reaction Network}
Methane pyrolysis produces carbon clusters:
\[
\text{CH}_4 \rightarrow \text{C} + 2\text{H}_2,
\]
\[
\text{CH}_4 \rightarrow \text{C}_2\text{H}_2 + \text{H}_2,
\]
\[
\text{C}_2\text{H}_2 \rightarrow \text{C}_2 + \text{H}_2.
\]

Cluster growth:
\[
\text{C}_n + \text{C} \rightarrow \text{C}_{n+1},
\]
with nucleation rates predicted by UUAS THERMO-Q2.

These reactions influence radiative emission and ablation chemistry.

\subsection{Radiative Emission Processes}
Shock-layer radiation is dominated by:
\begin{itemize}
    \item H and H$_2$ continuum emission,
    \item H$_3^+$ rovibrational lines,
    \item CH$_x$ band emission,
    \item C$_2$ Swan bands.
\end{itemize}

Radiative flux:
\[
q_{\text{rad}} = \int_0^\infty \kappa_\lambda B_\lambda(T)\, d\lambda,
\]
where $\kappa_\lambda$ is spectral absorption coefficient and
$B_\lambda$ is Planck function.

UUAS ION-Q4 provides $\kappa_\lambda$ for H$_3^+$ and CH$_x$ species.

\subsection{Chemical Energy Exchange}
Chemical energy absorption:
\[
q_{\text{chem}} = \sum_i \dot{\omega}_i h_i,
\]
where $h_i$ is species enthalpy.

UUAS predicts:
\[
q_{\text{chem}} \approx 3{-}5~\mathrm{MW/m^2},
\]
reducing stagnation-point temperature and ablation rate.

\subsection{Nonequilibrium Effects}
Nonequilibrium arises when:
\[
T_e \neq T_{\text{trans}} \neq T_{\text{vib}} \neq T_{\text{rot}},
\]
requiring multi-temperature modeling.

Vibrational relaxation:
\[
\frac{dT_{\text{vib}}}{dt}
= -\frac{T_{\text{vib}} - T_{\text{trans}}}{\tau_{\text{vib}}},
\]
with relaxation time $\tau_{\text{vib}}$ from THERMO-Q2.

\subsection{Coupling to Aerothermodynamics}
Shock-layer chemistry directly influences:
\begin{itemize}
    \item radiative heating $q_{\text{rad}}$,
    \item convective heating $q_{\text{conv}}$,
    \item ablation rate $\dot{m}_{\text{abl}}$,
    \item deceleration profile $a_D$,
    \item parachute deployment altitude $h_{\text{deploy}}$.
\end{itemize}

Chemical nonequilibrium reduces peak heating and modifies descent
dynamics.

\subsection{Conclusion}
Shock-layer chemistry during Uranus entry is dominated by hydrogen
dissociation, methane pyrolysis, ion formation, and nonequilibrium
radiative processes. These reactions significantly affect thermal
protection, deceleration, and descent stability. The derived chemical
framework aligns with UUAS THERMO-Q2 and ION-Q4 predictions and provides
a complete basis for modeling probe entry behavior.